## [ISSUE] Backup code + any `totpCode` skips the password check entirely

**Severity:** HIGH — one leaked backup code plus the email is enough to log in. The account is left with a single factor.

### The Problem

`authorize()` now decides the flow from two independent credential fields:

```ts
// line 121 (existing)
if (user.password && !credentials.totpCode) {
  const isCorrectPassword = await verifyPassword(credentials.password, user.password);
  ...
}

// line 131 (new)
if (user.twoFactorEnabled && credentials.backupCode) {
  ... // validates backup code only
} else if (user.twoFactorEnabled) {
  ... // validates totpCode
}
```

The password is only checked when `totpCode` is empty. Before this PR that was safe, because a non-empty `totpCode` always reached the `else if (user.twoFactorEnabled)` branch and had to be a valid TOTP. With this PR, a request that sets **both** `totpCode` (any value, e.g. `"000000"`) and `backupCode` does this:

1. Line 121: `!credentials.totpCode` is false, so the password check is skipped.
2. Line 131: `credentials.backupCode` is truthy, so the backup-code branch runs.
3. The TOTP branch is never reached, so the bogus `totpCode` is never checked.

Result: `POST /api/auth/callback/credentials` with `email`, `backupCode=<valid>`, `totpCode=000000`, and no password returns a session.

### Theory / Proof

A backup code is meant to stand in for the *second* factor only (NIST SP 800-63B §5.1.2, "look-up secrets", is a replacement authenticator for the possession factor, not for the memorized secret). Here it can replace both factors. The UI never sends both fields, but the NextAuth credentials endpoint accepts whatever fields a client posts, so the UI is not a security boundary.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Normal UI logins work, because `login.tsx` clears `totpCode` when switching to "Lost access". An attacker who gets one backup code (the PR tells users to download them to `cal-backup-codes.txt` or copy them to the clipboard) can log in without the password. With the password check skipped, backup-code guessing also needs nothing but the email, though it is still limited by the rate limiter and the 40-bit code space.

### Research

GitHub, Google and the `django-otp` static-token device all check backup codes only *after* the primary credential has been verified. None of them lets a backup code short-circuit the password.

### Recommendation

Always require the password unless this is the external-IdP TOTP flow, and never let a backup code skip it:

```ts
const usingBackupCode = user.twoFactorEnabled && !!credentials.backupCode;

if (user.password && (usingBackupCode || !credentials.totpCode)) {
  const isCorrectPassword = await verifyPassword(credentials.password, user.password);
  if (!isCorrectPassword) throw new Error(ErrorCode.IncorrectEmailPassword);
}
```

Or reject requests that carry both fields:

```ts
if (credentials.backupCode && credentials.totpCode) throw new Error(ErrorCode.IncorrectEmailPassword);
```
